+ * packet can be answered without reading a size the sender chose. That is the right default for a
+ * peer that has nothing to say about the channel, but it makes plugin messaging impossible: a
+ * modded client sends its real data over a channel the server has no codec for <em>by design</em>,
+ * and a body discarded on arrival means a server-side plugin can never hear it.
+ *
+ * <p><b>Carried as a patch rather than worked around.</b> The body has to be part of the value — it
+ * is what the decoder produces and the encoder consumes, and a record's components are fixed at
+ * compile time, so neither a mixin nor a subclass can add it. The size limit is what keeps the
+ * guarantee vanilla cared about: the length is still bounded by {@code maxPayloadSize} before a
+ * byte is read, so an oversized payload is rejected rather than allocated.
